### 7.3 Evolutionary Optimization Process

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Input["UI payload: groups, tableCapacity, alpha, p"]
    Normalize["`_prepare_solver_groups`"]
    Tree["`GuestTree` + weighted distance matrix"]
    Init["Initial population\nrandom / nearest-neighbor / top-down / bottom-up / largest-first / optional GNN"]
    NSGA["NSGA-II loop in `optimizer.py`"]
    Mutate["Mutation operators"]
    Cross["Subtree crossover + repair"]
    Fitness["Objectives:\n1. number of tables\n2. alpha * loneliness - cohesion"]
    Stop["Stop when convergence, generation cap, or time cap is reached"]
    Front["Keep best penalty per table count"]
    Clean["`_clean_assignment` -> chunk list returned by API"]

    Input --> Normalize --> Tree --> Init --> NSGA
    Tree --> Fitness
    NSGA --> Mutate --> Fitness --> NSGA
    NSGA --> Cross --> Fitness
    NSGA --> Stop --> Front --> Clean
```

The runtime code therefore does not expose the raw NSGA-II population. It reduces the result to one best solution per table count before sending the response back to the UI.

